<%= render "shared/page_header",
      title: "Behavior",
      intro: "Elephants are intelligent, social animals that live in close family groups, communicate in ways we're still learning about, and look after one another." %>

<div class="section">
  <h2>How elephants get food</h2>
  <ul class="fact-list">
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Trunk</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Wraps around grass to pull it up, plucks fruit and leaves, and reaches branches up to 6 m high.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Tusks</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Strip bark from trees and dig up roots.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Feet</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Kick loose clumps of grass and dig into dry riverbeds to find water.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Strength</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Push over whole trees to reach the leaves and fruit at the top.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Memory</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Matriarchs remember where food and water are found in each season, sometimes decades later.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>How elephants protect themselves from predators</h2>
  <p>
    Healthy adults have few natural predators because of their size. Calves, however, can be attacked by lions, hyenas and crocodiles in Africa,
    or tigers in Asia. Humans, through poaching and habitat loss, are by far the biggest threat.
  </p>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Strength in numbers</h3>
      <p>When threatened, the herd forms a defensive circle with the calves safely in the middle.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Warning displays</h3>
      <p>Spread ears, a raised trunk, loud trumpeting and mock charges are usually enough to scare predators away.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Tusks and size</h3>
      <p>If a threat doesn't back down, an elephant can charge at around 25 km/h and fight back with its tusks and its sheer weight.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Alarm calls</h3>
      <p>Elephants make different alarm calls for different dangers. Some research suggests they have a specific call for humans.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Sleeping habits</h2>
  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="stat-label">In the wild</div>
      <div class="stat-value">~2 hours</div>
      <p>Of sleep a day, mostly in the early morning.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="stat-label">In zoos</div>
      <div class="stat-value">4–6 hours</div>
      <p>Of sleep a night, when there are no predators to watch for.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="info-block">
    <p>
      Wild elephants sleep less than almost any other mammal. They usually sleep standing up and lie down only every few days for deeper sleep.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p>
      <strong>Record breakers:</strong> a study of matriarchs in Botswana found they sometimes stayed awake for up to 46 hours straight,
      often while moving away from predators or poachers.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Other fascinating behaviours</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Family life</h3>
      <p>Herds are made up of related females and their young, led by a matriarch. Males leave as teenagers and live alone or in "bachelor" groups.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Communication</h3>
      <p>Low rumbles, many too deep for humans to hear, can travel several kilometres through the air and the ground.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Mourning</h3>
      <p>Elephants have been seen gently touching the bones of dead elephants and standing quietly near them, as if grieving.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
